{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 1, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187 he was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. \n*Early Life* \nhe was born around 1140 and was the heir to the County of Tripoli he became the Count of Tripoli after his father's death in 1152. \nRaymond III Count of Tripoli played an important role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the 12th century he was a member of the Haute Cour of Jerusalem and participated in the council that decided to launch a crusade against the Fatimid Caliphate in Egypt. \n*Reign* \nhe was a skilled military leader and led several campaigns against the Muslim states in the region including the Zengid dynasty and the Fatimid Caliphate he also played a key role in the Battle of Inab in 1149 where the Crusader forces were defeated by the Zengid dynasty. \nRaymond III Count of Tripoli was also involved in the internal politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and supported the claims of Constance of Antioch to the throne of the Principality of Antioch he also supported the claims of Amalric I of Jerusalem to the throne of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. \n*Marriage and Children* \nhe married Hodierna of Jerusalem's niece Eschiva of Bures and had two daughters with her Alice of Tripoli and Melisende of Tripoli. \nRaymond III Count of Tripoli died in 1187 and was succeeded by his nephew Raymond IV Count of Tripoli he was remembered as a skilled military leader and a shrewd politician who played an important role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the 12th century. \n*Capture and Later Life* \nhe was captured by the forces of Nur ad-Din Zengi in 1164 and was held prisoner for several years he was eventually released and returned to the County of Tripoli where he continued to rule until his death in 1187. \nRaymond III Count of Tripoli was also known for his conflicts with the Knights Templar and the Hospitaller Knights he saw them as a threat to his power and tried to limit their influence in the County of Tripoli. \n*Legacy* \nhe was remembered as a skilled military leader and a shrewd politician who played an important role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the 12th century his legacy continued to shape the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem for centuries after his death.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -1.9073468138230965e-06}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.0009117737063206732}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.0004573014739435166}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.4371652603149414}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.6498938202857971}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -8.22540732769994e-06}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.933898824470816e-06}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0988910049200058}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -8.702239938429557e-06}, {"token": "115", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -2.145764938177308e-06}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.005236481316387653}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -7.867782187531702e-06}, {"token": "118", "logprob": -1.7881377516459906e-06}, {"token": "7", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.07735174894332886}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.010510919615626335}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.34882110357284546}, {"token": " son", "logprob": -0.023079462349414825}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Raymond", "logprob": -9.894321920000948e-06}, {"token": " II", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.004352263640612364}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -1.5